#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152923} :activity/id #uuid "fb0fa30b-881f-56fa-a0e3-9ebae5ac20bd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048577 :topic/id #uuid "aae1aef6-2b7b-5139-9696-8862a8c42ce4" :topic/math-academy-id 2793} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152923 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153432 :tutorial/content "Consider the truth table for $S \\Rightarrow N$ below. Note that the implication is true $({\\color{blue}\\textrm{T}})$ in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th rows.\n\n| $S$ | $N$ | $S \\Rightarrow N$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}\\textrm T$ | $\\color{red}\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue}\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue}\\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue}\\textrm T$ |\n\nOf the three rows where the implication is true, $S$ is true $({\\color{red}\\textrm{T}})$ only in the $1$st row, and $N$ is also true $({\\color{red}\\textrm{T}})$ in this row.\n\nSo, if the implication is true, $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is called a **sufficient condition** for $N$.\n\nOn the other hand, if $S$ is true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is also true. Therefore, $N$ is called a **necessary condition** for $S$.\nHowever, $N$ being true *does not guarantee* that $S$ is true, as we can see from the $3$rd row in the truth table.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textbf{S}\\textrm{ufficient} \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\textbf{N}\\textrm{ecessary}\n$$\n\nLet's look at some examples:\n\n- Consider the following true implication:\n$a > 0\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a^2 > 0$\nWe can label each part of the statement as follows:\n$\\underbrace{\\big(\\, a > 0 \\, \\big)}_{\\large\\textrm{sufficient}} \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\underbrace{\\big(\\, a^2 > 0 \\, \\big)}_{\\large\\textrm{necessary}}$\nTherefore:$a > 0$ is a *sufficient* condition for $a^2 > 0$. In other words, $a > 0$ *guarantees* that $a^2 > 0$.$a^2 > 0$ is a *necessary* condition for $a > 0$. In other words, if $a > 0$, it is necessary that $a^2 > 0$. However, $a^2 > 0$ *does not guarantee* that $a > 0$.\n- Suppose your teacher promises that *if* you score at least $90$ on the exam, *then* you will receive an $A$. We can express this promise as the following implication:\n$90$\nLet's assume the teacher is telling the truth, so the implication is true. We can label each part of our implication as follows:\n$90$\nTherefore:Scoring at least $90$ on the exam is a *sufficient* condition for getting an $A$. In other words, scoring at least $90$ *guarantees* that you'll get an $A$.Getting an $A$ is a *necessary* condition for scoring at least $90$. In other words, if you score at least $90$, it necessarily follows that you'll receive $A$. However, getting an $A$ *does not guarantee* that you scored at least $90$ on the exam. For example, the teacher may have decided to award an $A$ despite a score smaller than 90 if the exam was particularly tough that year." :tutorial/id #uuid "3e91a89c-375e-5c19-8d50-7a5d47800579" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8001 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e11d65a7-2521-5479-b285-2b79997d9e03" :step/math-academy-id 144950 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152924}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152924 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152076 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151816 :question/id #uuid "2f4fba37-0a5e-5039-8b70-a42bfb1f93ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-12758"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6414abb9-00d3-55ea-a1e5-da99999c3ccc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048574}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152457}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary or Sufficient Conditions From Implications"} :step/id #uuid "c38c14fb-17e9-5f85-991b-ac6fcc11938e" :step/math-academy-id 144951 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152925}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152925 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152077 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151814 :question/id #uuid "a4f2cac5-8a54-5c65-8e53-7098f15ac583" :question/math-academy-id "e-12754"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bc3ecd-2da1-5951-abba-95da02dee826" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152453}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary Conditions"} :step/id #uuid "8b0274e4-9379-5bd8-af56-75d05a2a3640" :step/math-academy-id 144954 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152926}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152926 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152078 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151815 :question/id #uuid "aa1bcb9c-64a8-5778-9624-5dd9f0d7b98f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12755"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7345a2-810f-5a35-aa01-092e40df06f3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050558}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152455}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sufficient Conditions"} :step/id #uuid "48e01a17-a585-5104-b9a4-bafd11c635c7" :step/math-academy-id 144958 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152927}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152927 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153444 :tutorial/content "Suppose that an equivalence $P \\Leftrightarrow Q$ is true. Then, we have the following terminology:\n\n- $P$ is a **necessary and sufficient condition** for $Q$\n- $Q$ is a **necessary and sufficient condition** for $P$\n\nFor example, consider the following two predicates:\n\n- $35 \\mid n$\n- $5 \\mid n$ and $7 \\mid n$\n\nThese are both necessary and sufficient conditions for each other.\n\nIndeed, recall that a number is divisible by $35$ *if and only if* it's divisible by both $5$ and $7$. In other words, we have\n\n$$\n5∣n\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n5∣n\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n5∣n\n$$\n\nIn words:\n\n- $\\big(35 \\mid n \\big)$ *is necessary and sufficient for* $5∣n$\n- $5∣n$ *is necessary and sufficient for* $\\big(35 \\mid n \\big)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "87478f3c-00be-545c-b9ce-1618a66bf0b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8321 :tutorial/title "Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"} :step/id #uuid "8943f5ca-c1e2-5d7c-afb1-72c562a7f753" :step/math-academy-id 144962 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152928}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152928 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152079 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151831 :question/id #uuid "2d5bef9d-83c7-5a4d-9cda-cdf0c8c9cd3c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13272"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "655a0356-bbf0-5769-86d7-6674c65d0f87" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050073}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152469}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary and Sufficient Conditions"} :step/id #uuid "c964453f-f719-562b-a613-a11fb6b95bfd" :step/math-academy-id 144963}] :activity/title "Necessary and Sufficient Conditions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152377}]}
